WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.650
let's get into your questions for the week.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.180
What's a deal, Blake?

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:12.309
Breaker David, when voting where you simply
will not vote for someone if they have a certain

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:13.309
our

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:17.449
view for or against something or do a certain
thing.

00:00:17.449 --> 00:00:19.240
So I'll answer this.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:26.289
It's important that we differentiate between
litmus tests, deal breakers, and sort of being

00:00:26.289 --> 00:00:29.930
like a single issue voter because these are
all different things.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:36.700
I've talked before about the way in which
litmus tests can be damaging to getting the

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:42.290
worst person out of office or preventing the
worst candidate from winning.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:47.480
When litmus tests are used to divide a movement
in a way that makes it fail to do what the

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.580
bigger goal sort of is.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:55.790
Um, what, what I think it's damaging to do
is to go from thinking about deal-breakers

00:00:55.790 --> 00:01:02.010
in a reasonable way to litmus tests that are
sort of out of context and sometimes self-defeating

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:07.680
to the type of single issue mentality that
can almost make you a damaging participant

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.130
in the system in the context of achieving
broader goals.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:15.900
Also, I think that what is a deal breaker
in a primary election can be different than

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.300
what is a deal breaker in a general election.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.850
Uh, and I think, you know, the, the centrist
establishment Democrats like Joe Biden, the

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.370
latest version of Pete boot edge edge, because
in 2018 his rhetoric was far more leftist,

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.050
Amy Klobuchar, et cetera.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.600
I would still vote for them in the general
election.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.850
So I think that that's important to consider.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.310
Often for me, the things that feel like deal
breakers where I just become so disgusted

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:44.880
with a candidate that I just won't even consider
them the sort of WTF moments.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.220
It's social issues.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.910
And what's interesting about that is that
economic issues are the ones that are much

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:53.650
more impactful to a greater number of people
in many ways.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:59.590
But when I find out, like if you think gay
people shouldn't be married, uh, that's a

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:03.860
drastically different thing than we disagree
with the top marginal tax rate would be.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:08.700
Now, interestingly enough, what the top marginal
tax rate is and the government revenue that

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:14.450
comes from that and what can be done with
that money may actually impact way more people

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:18.129
than whether a candidate believes that gay
and lesbian couples should or shouldn't be

00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:19.409
able to get married.

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:25.999
But at this point it is so whacked to me to
still be saying, I'm really against, uh, two

00:02:25.999 --> 00:02:27.999
guys getting married or two women getting
married.

00:02:27.999 --> 00:02:30.310
There's no sensible reason for that.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:33.260
We, there is debate about what the top marginal
tax rate should be.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:37.079
Now, if you want to come to me and say the
rich should pay no taxes and the poor should

00:02:37.079 --> 00:02:41.499
pay huge taxes, that's so stupid that maybe
it would become a deal breaker.

00:02:41.499 --> 00:02:48.311
But there is a, uh, there is a debate to be
had about where the top marginal tax rates

00:02:48.311 --> 00:02:50.340
should be and what the tax brackets should
be.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.900
And it's not a deal breaker in that cartoonish
way.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:57.180
Somebody who believes the Bible should be
the law of the land.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:02.499
That seems like a deal breaker to me and it's
hard to imagine someone just coming out and

00:03:02.499 --> 00:03:03.499
saying it.

00:03:03.499 --> 00:03:07.419
It's usually intimated or implied through
positions on abortion or gay marriage or,

00:03:07.419 --> 00:03:08.840
or whatever else.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.419
You can't think climate change is just a fake
hoax, right?

00:03:12.419 --> 00:03:14.389
That that's a deal breaker.

00:03:14.389 --> 00:03:17.379
You could disagree about how we should deal
with climate change.

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:22.329
You can disagree about how we should deal
with carbon carbon credits versus, you know

00:03:22.329 --> 00:03:25.879
what, we could have that debate, but you can't
think that it's made up.

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:27.290
That is a deal breaker.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.209
But really it's all context.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:33.519
We've talked on the program before about how
it's really important to know what our principles

00:03:33.519 --> 00:03:40.209
are, but those who just focus on the principals
often are unable to actually have pragmatic

00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:42.980
conversations and make pragmatic decisions.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.840
When you have to apply the principles to the
real world.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.419
Eventually you're choosing between two or
a few people in terms of who's likely to actually

00:03:51.419 --> 00:03:53.590
represent you in a candidacy.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:59.239
The principles are important as a starting
point and eventually you would try to apply

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:04.249
those principles to the positions of the candidate
and concessions likely have to be made.

00:04:04.249 --> 00:04:08.299
And so I think it's great to understand what
are the deal breakers for you.

00:04:08.299 --> 00:04:12.599
You don't want to go over into sort of toxic
litmus test world where you end up damaging

00:04:12.599 --> 00:04:18.090
your own movement and eventually you almost
always are going to have to make some compromises

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.209
on whoever it is you choose to support.

